In-place upgrades for single-file TiddlyWikis from the browser.

This plugin can upload tiddlywiki starting from version 5.3.2. If your tiddlywiki is older, 
<details>
<summary>follow these steps.</summary>
<p>

# Click on this button to export your wiki: <<export-btn>>
# Drag and drop the export in the official upgrade wizard: <iframe src="https://tiddlywiki.com/upgrade.html" style="height:500px;width:100%;border:none"/>
# Check that the upgraded wiki works properly then manually upload it to your hosting service.

</p>
</details>

!! Features

* Upgrade to the latest stable release or prerelease.
* Works with standard TiddlyWiki savers.
* Supports external-core wikis.
* Optionally downloads an HTML backup before upgrading.
* Preserves edits made after an upgrade and before reloading.
* Asks for explicit confirmation before making any changes, with an extra warning if backups are disabled.
* Attempts to confirm that the save actually succeeded before offering to reload.

!! How It Works

The `$action-inplace-upgrade` widget:

# Asks for confirmation before proceeding. If automatic backup is disabled, a second, more explicit warning is shown.
# Optionally downloads an HTML backup of the complete current wiki, using its configured save template. If the backup fails to start, the upgrade is aborted.
# Fetches the target `empty.html` shell.
# Parses the fetched shell as an HTML document and verifies it contains exactly one tiddler store (`script.tiddlywiki-tiddler-store`). If none or more than one is found, the upgrade is aborted.
# Exports selected source tiddlers, merges them with any required configuration (see below), escapes `<` as `\u003c`, and inserts them as a new `<script class="tiddlywiki-tiddler-store">` element immediately after the target's own store node.
# For external-core targets, resolves the absolute core script URL from the single matching `script[src*="tiddlywikicore"]` element and injects it into `$:/config/inplace-upgrade/core-url`. The plugin's packaged `coreURL` macro reads this configuration.
# Delivers the assembled HTML to the active saver via the standard `tm-save-wiki` mechanism.
# Waits (up to 20 seconds) to observe the saver's own success or failure signal:
## If a save failure is detected, the upgrade is rolled back and your original wiki is left unchanged.
## If success is confirmed, a refresh action is shown.
## If the outcome cannot be determined automatically (e.g. some network or download savers), you're asked to manually confirm the save completed before reloading.
# Until reload, intercepts normal saves and rebuilds the upgraded HTML from the current tiddlers. Edits and new tiddlers made after the upgrade are therefore retained.

The target's original store remains intact. During boot, TiddlyWiki loads both JSON stores in document order, so exported tiddlers override same-titled target tiddlers.

!! Installation

Drag and drop the plugin into your wiki, save, and reload.

!! Notes

Your themes and third-party plugins are exported with the selected tiddlers. A plugin incompatible with the target core can prevent the upgraded wiki from booting correctly.

The widget requires a browser environment and a target URL that is accessible through `fetch()`. Remote targets must permit CORS; local `file://` wikis may require a relative target URL or a local web server.

Save-completion detection relies on observing TiddlyWiki's standard success notification and failure alert. Some savers (notably certain network/upload savers) may not surface either signal in a way the widget can detect; in that case the upgrade is marked "unconfirmed" rather than assumed successful, and you should verify the save genuinely completed (e.g. via the saver's own confirmation) before using the refresh button.

You will always be asked to confirm before anything is modified, and if backups are enabled, a failed backup attempt stops the whole upgrade rather than silently continuing without one.